{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdYZ9HK9WSj9Y6cRMGcE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"On October 29, OpenAI published an update to its Model Spec, the public document describing how the company wants its models to behave. The guidance is now organized around five values: faithfulness to human intent, harm minimization, honesty and transparency, human agency, and care. The revision adds guidance on context, oversight and agency, adjusts some behavioral defaults, and retires most of the detailed style guidance. Outside a set of firm safety boundaries, the Spec mostly consists of defaults that users and developers can override. It also now refers to the model as \"GPT\" where it used to say \"the assistant.\"\n\nThe change with the most practical weight is how the Spec treats instructions. The new section on faithfulness starts from the premise that what people value is primary, and that instructions are ordinarily the clearest and most important signals of those values. OpenAI says this approach borrows from the assistance-games framing of alignment. The section includes an updated chain of command that sets out which instructions apply and how conflicts get resolved. A new subsection, \"consider context carefully,\" describes how conversations, memories and other contextual evidence should inform the model's understanding of what someone intends and prefers. OpenAI's stated reason is that models are taking more consequential actions and need to exercise judgment in situations their instructions don't explicitly cover.\n\nGiving a model more room to infer intent also gives it more room to infer wrongly, and the Spec pairs that latitude with two checks. The first is visibility. In OpenAI's example, a model helping someone compare job offers might draw on a priority they shared before, such as predictable hours, while making that assumption visible and easy to correct. The second appears in the decision on style. OpenAI wants style to adapt to people's preferences, in aggregate, within languages and cultures, and for each person, using feedback and context as evidence. It keeps stronger defaults such as approaching questions objectively. Users and developers can explicitly ask the model to take a particular perspective, but the model should not infer that preference merely from what it thinks they want to hear. As I read it, context is allowed to shape how the model writes, while a change in the perspective it takes requires an explicit request.\n\nAll of this describes intended behavior. OpenAI says its models do not yet fully reflect the Spec. It is exploring new training methods meant to help models learn these values and apply them in new situations, and says it will share results in future updates. The update tells us how OpenAI wants these trade-offs resolved. It does not yet show whether models will reliably surface the assumptions they draw from memory, or decline to tell people what they want to hear. The forthcoming training results should be judged on those two behaviors."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3678,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3678},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"58b275fb-895b-4eb8-acee-0f53ea4a84d1","uuid":"96e719d1-4e24-415c-9e24-1c530de1e26c","timestamp":"2026-11-02T05:25:23.960Z","request_id":"req_011CgdYZ95Qi1PQq4e7YQnaV"}
